{"opening": "Call 911 now. A gas smell is an emergency. Do not wait for a landlord response or stay in the building if the smell is strong.", "record": "This building at 2 Fixture Ave, Brooklyn is a multiple dwelling with 8 residential units, built in 1930. It currently has 1 open class-C violation (most serious), 1 open class-B violation, and no class-A violations (HPD dataset, verified 2026-10-31). Class-C violations are immediately hazardous.", "law": "NYC Administrative Code § 27-2005(a) requires that "The owner of a multiple dwelling shall keep the premises in good repair." 

NYC Administrative Code § 27-2005(f) requires the owner to deliver to each tenant a notice about gas leak procedures that instructs tenants to "first call 911 and then call the relevant gas service provider... before contacting such owner or an agent thereof when a gas leak is suspected."

The New York Court system confirms that Housing Court handles emergency repairs: see NY Courts' HP Cases guidance at https://www.nycourts.gov/new-york-city-housing-court/hp-cases-obtain-repairs.", "inference": "The statute requires owners of this building to keep it in good repair and places gas emergencies outside the normal repair-request process. The law itself directs you to emergency services (911) and the gas company first. After the emergency is resolved, any damage may be a repair the landlord owes under § 27-2005(a), depending on what the emergency services find.", "question": "After 911 and the gas company respond to the smell at 2 Fixture Ave, can I file an HP case to compel the landlord to repair any damage or hazard under § 27-2005(a)?", "help": "Call The Legal Aid Society's Brooklyn office at 718-722-3100 after the emergency is controlled. They provide free legal assistance in housing matters and can advise on what to do after 911 responds. Bring documentation from the gas company and emergency responders about what they found and any repairs they recommend."}

{"record": "This question is not about 1 Fixture Ave, Brooklyn. Good Cause Eviction is a state law that applies statewide, including in New York City.", "law": "Under New York's Good Cause Eviction (GCE) law, which is mandatory in New York City, an annual rent increase above **the lower of (a) 5 percent plus the annual percentage change in the consumer price index for the region, or (b) 10 percent** is presumptively unreasonable (NY Real Property Law § 231-c, cited in the state budget).

The GCE law applies to rental units whose monthly rent does not exceed 245% of the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development's fair market rent for the county and unit size (NYS DHCR, Good Cause Eviction Law Required DHCR Notice, July 2026). DHCR publishes the dollar thresholds each year.

When a landlord increases rent above this threshold for a covered unit, the 14-day rent demand notice must state the landlord's justification for the increase (NY Real Property Actions and Proceedings Law § 711 and NY RPL § 231-c).", "inference": "The law sets a numerical cap—either 10% or 5% plus inflation, whichever is lower—that presumptively protects tenants against very large annual increases. If a landlord proposes an increase above that cap for a covered unit, the law does not forbid it, but requires the landlord to state a justification in writing, and any eviction proceeding for nonpayment would need to address whether the increase is reasonable given that justification. Whether your unit is covered depends on its current rent relative to fair market rent for your county.", "question": "Is a rent increase above 10 percent (or 5 percent plus the annual CPI for my region, whichever is lower) presumptively unreasonable under NY Real Property Law § 231-c if my unit is covered by Good Cause Eviction?", "help": "Met Council on Housing — Tenants Rights Hotline, 212-979-0611, Monday & Wednesday 1:30pm–8pm & Friday 1:30pm–5pm. Before you call, gather your current lease, recent rent receipts or bank records, any rent increase notice you received, and today's rent amount so they can advise whether your unit is covered (it depends on your current rent vs. the fair market rent threshold for your county)."}

{"record": "This question is not about a specific building.", "law": "New York law requires that **within 14 days after the tenant has vacated the premises, the landlord shall provide the tenant with an itemized statement indicating the basis for the amount of the deposit retained, if any, and shall return any remaining portion of the deposit to the tenant** (NY General Obligations Law § 7-107 and § 7-108, https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GOB/7-107 and https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GOB/7-108).

**If a landlord fails to provide the tenant with the statement and deposit within 14 days, the landlord shall forfeit any right to retain any portion of the deposit.**

A landlord may keep only the amount legally allowed for unpaid rent, utilities, damages beyond normal wear and tear, and moving and storage of the tenant's belongings. If the landlord withholds any part of the security deposit, they must provide an itemized list of damages and the cost of repairs (NYS DHCR Fact Sheet #9, https://hcr.ny.gov/system/files/documents/2025/11/fact-sheet-09-11-2025.pdf).", "inference": "The 14-day deadline is a hard deadline: if the landlord misses it without providing both the itemized statement and the remaining deposit, the law says they forfeit the right to retain any of the deposit. This is a significant protection—it is worth confirming whether the statement you received is itemized and specific enough under the law's requirement before the deadline passes.", "question": "If my landlord does not return my deposit and provide an itemized statement within 14 days of move-out, what should I do to enforce my rights under § 7-107 or § 7-108?", "help": "Met Council on Housing's Tenants Rights Hotline at 212-979-0611 (Monday & Wednesday 1:30pm–8pm, Friday 1:30pm–5pm) can give you free brief answers or referrals. To document your case, keep your lease and a receipt or bank record of the deposit paid, dated photographs of the apartment when you moved in and when you moved out, and every written message with the landlord about the deposit with its date."}
